[Music]  hi I'm Nicolas be with reason TV today  my guest is salon comms  Glenn Greenwald the author of several  best-selling books including how would a  patriot act and a tragic legacy Glenn  has also just authored a Cato Institute  policy study on drug decriminalization  and Portugal and the lessons that we can  take from this in virtually completely  successful experiment let's talk about  drug decriminalization in Portugal your  paper is lessons for creating fair and  successful policies first tell us what  is the difference in Portugal between  decriminalization and legalization  well the difference in in general is is  the idea that legalization means that  there are no longer any legal  prohibitions of any kind on drug  consumption usage or possession whereas  in a decriminalize framework the law  continues to prohibit drug usage but  it's completely removed from the  criminal sphere so that if you violate  that prohibition or do the activity that  the law says you cannot do you're no  longer committing a crime you cannot be  turned into a criminal by the state  instead it's deemed to be an  administrative offence only and you're  put into an administrative proceeding  rather than a criminal proceeding  explained very quickly what what  happened in Portugal well what's  interesting is the impetus behind  decriminalization which took place in  2001 was not that there was some drive  to have a sort of libertarian ideology  perfected based on the idea that adults  should be able to use whatever  substances they want nor was it because  there's some adil ik upper middle class  setting I mean it looks Portugal was a  very poor country it's not Luxembourg or  Monaco or something like that and in  fact in the 1990s they had a spiraling  out of control drug problem addiction  was skyrocketing drug related  pathologies were increasing rapidly and  they were sort of taking this step out  of desperation and they convened a  Council of a political policy experts  and gave them the mandate to determine  which the which optimal policy approach  would enable them to best deal with  these these drug problems and the  council convened and studied all the  various options and decriminalization  was the answer to the question how can  we best  limit drug usage and drug addiction and  it was a policy designed to do that and  one of the things that you find is that  in fact decriminalization which removes  a certain amount of sanction from drug  use it actually correlates with less  drug use absolutely and you can look at  the empirical evidence which is quite  compelling and unambiguous in many  senses and in in a lot of demographic  groups especially the key demographic  groups in Portugal 15 to 19 years old  for instance from 2001 when  decriminalization was institutionalized  until say 2007 there has been a decrease  in absolute terms in the prevalence  rates of drug usages what explains that  because I mean a kind of basic theory  would say if you make drugs less if you  lower the cost of doing drugs by making  a less criminally offensive you would  have more of it well there's several  aspects one is the concern that  policymakers had the frustration in the  1990s when they were criminalizing is  the more they criminalized the more the  usage rates went up and one of the  reasons was was because when you tell  the population that you will imprison  them or treat them as criminals if they  identify themselves as drug users or you  learned that they're using drugs what  you do is you create a barrier between  the government and the citizenry such  that their citizenry fears the  government which means that government  officials can't offer treatment programs  they can't communicate with the  population effectively they can't offer  them services and so there was this  barrier that drug officials had and  couldn't overcome and criminalization  was in place but more importantly when  you spend all your money on arresting  and prosecuting and imprisoning people  for drug usage it means there's no money  for things like funding treatment or  educational campaigns or harm reduction  programs and once Portugal decriminalize  a huge amount of money that had gone to  putting its citizens into cages was  freed up and it enabled the government  to provide meaningful treatment to  people who wanted it and so addicts were  able to turn into non drug users and  usage rates went down what is the what's  the relevance for the United States we  have a incredibly vexed history with  drug policy we also have much higher  than average drug usage rates we have  more people in prison because of drugs  etc well I mean the I think the the  relevance is obvious which is we have  debates all the time now about things  like drug policy reform and  criminalisation and it's based purely in  speculation and fear-mongering of all  the horrible things that are supposedly  going to happen if we loosen our drug  laws and we can remove ourselves from  the realm of the speculative by looking  at Portugal which actually  decriminalized seven years ago in full  every drug and see that none of those  parade of Horrible's that's constantly  warned of by decriminalization opponents  actually came to fruition Lisbon didn't  turn into a drug haven for drug tourists  the explosive explosion and drug usage  rates that was predicted never  materialized in fact the opposite  happened and interestingly the populace  was split before decriminalization as to  whether it was a good idea eight years  ago and whereas now there's a virtual  consensus across the political spectrum  in Portugal that decriminalization has  acceded and there's no movement to  return to the criminalized framework  let's switch gears a little bit at salon  as well as in your book you write a lot  about civil liberties you were an  unsparing critic of George W Bush for  all totally understandable reasons on  civil liberties how is Obama shaping up  on civil liberties not very good I think  that the some of the signs are still  inconclusive for example there was a  flurry of symbolic executive orders in  the first week designed to placate civil  libertarians excluding yourself  including myself but it didn't it worked  for about a day or so um although it was  clear from the beginning that most of  the difficult questions were put off so  if you close Guantanamo in a year that's  a marginally good thing but then if you  if you retain the right or the claimed  right to detain people without due  process or worse you create perniciously  named national security courts that are  by design intended to constrict due  process and make it easier to detain  people without having to prove their  guilt and you move that into the United  States you actually transplant  Guantanamo from Cuba into United States  that actually could make things a lot  worse and they've retained a lot of  executive power theories that Bagram  detainees have no habeas corpus rights  that state secrets enable the President  to bar entire lawsuits even ones that  challenge his legal identities he's been  slow to release various torture memos  right yeah that's right in fact there  are three in particular that are  supposedly the most graphic detail and  graphic not in terms of intelligence  disclosures but in terms of authorizing  what are clearly were  crimes that are still concealed in the  EC Lu has continuously been battling  what is it what is guiding Obama I mean  I think all of us expected that Obama  would be better on civil liberties I'm  better on executive power than Bush have  been what is what is guiding his  decision-making on these types of issues  I mean one can only speculate of course  but I think that what is a principal  concern is that the more disclosures  there are about how extreme the  government's conduct was during the Bush  era the more the pressure will increase  both domestically and especially  internationally to actually do something  about it I mean if the evidence is  graphic and sufficiently vivid that our  government created it committed war  crimes brutal war crimes how do you  justify while paying lip service to the  rule of law the fact that your own  government successful government isn't  investigating or prosecuting and I think  they have a paralyzing political fear of  what will happen if they're forced into  investigating or prosecuting and so one  of the things they're trying to do is  use these secrecy instruments to keep  all of that behind the wall of secrecy  that the Bush administration operated by  Obama on drug policy during the campaign  he said he would end medical marijuana  raids on medical marijuana dispensaries  in states where they're legal Eric  Holder is AG said yes they were gonna do  that there have been a couple of raids  since then do you think Obama will be  good on drug policy or fair to middlin  well I mean again I think things are  quite preliminary but I thought it was  actually rather discouraging um one  could even say infuriating to watch him  respond to the question that people who  he invited online to submit questions  and vote on them it decided was the most  important one he sort of scoffed at the  question about drug legalization kind of  reacted like an adolescent would  giggling and snickering at the very  mention of marijuana and I don't think  anyone expected him to advocate  legalization but the idea that he  reinforced the notion that it was a  frivolous concern that the only people  interested would be people who want to  get high or otherwise freakish I thought  was a very discouraging sign and and as  you say the DEA in the last week rated a  perfectly legitimate medical marijuana  dispensary in San Francisco that had a  permit from the Health Department you  know going in with guns  and rating it and and it seemed to be a  violation of that policy as well so I  don't think the signs are very  encouraging initially well maybe we'll  meet again in Portugal though that'd be  great yeah we can take advantage of the  drug laws and do some field research  thank you very much our guest has been  Glenn Greenwald of salon.com who has  authored a Cato Institute public policy  paper on drug decriminalization and  Portugal thanks again for coming  [Music]

